## Why This Is Asked
The tokenized explanation approach isolates training data exposure, supporting privacy and auditability for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- JWT-based explanation tokens, HS256 signing
- model_versioning, token rotation, expiry
- input validation and per-user audit

## Code Example
```javascript
// Issue explain token (Node.js)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
function issueExplainToken(user_id, model_version, expiryMs, secret) {
  const payload = { sub: user_id, mv: model_version, exp: Math.floor(Date.now()/1000) + expiryMs/1000 };
  return jwt.sign(payload, secret, { algorithm: 'HS256', audience: user_id });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate signing keys and invalidate tokens after a model version update?
- How would you test for token forgery or replay attacks?

## Why This Is Asked

Adds a practical multi-tenant privacy control layer to a beginner API, highlighting data isolation, per-tenant budgets, and simple governance in a real system.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Per-tenant privacy budgets (epsilon, delta)
- Header validation (X-tenant-id)
- Tenant-scoped configuration
- Observability and audits

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: tenant-aware config lookup (Express)
function tenantMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (!tenant) return res.status(400).send('Missing X-tenant-id');
  req.tenant = loadTenantConfig(tenant); // returns {epsilon, delta, ...}
  if (!req.tenant) return res.status(403).send('Unknown tenant');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for cross-tenant data leakage?
- How would you migrate tenants to per-tenant budgets with zero downtime?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design offline-first features, idempotency, and conflict handling for mobile apps. Challenges include eventual consistency, sync retries, and safe canary rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first architecture
- Idempotent API design and deduplication
- Conflict resolution with timestamped records
- Data model versioning and canary controls
- Local testing strategies for offline/sync workflows

## Code Example
```javascript
function mergeScore(local, incoming) {
  // last write wins by client_timestamp per user
  if (incoming.client_timestamp > local.client_timestamp) {
    return { ...local, ...incoming };
  }
  return local;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test offline queue size and eviction policy?
- How would you handle partial failures during batch sync?

## Why This Is Asked
Schema evolution is common in production systems. This tests ability to plan backward-compatible changes that don’t break existing clients.

## Key Concepts
- API versioning and data contracts
- Migration scripts and defaulting
- Feature flags and canary rollouts
- Regression and contract tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Migration mapping
function migrate(payload) {
  if (payload.score_source) return payload;
  if (payload.model_version >= 2) payload.score_source = 'model';
  else payload.score_source = 'heuristic';
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rollback a failed migration?
- Which tests best validate both old and new clients?

## Why This Is Asked
This explores multi-tenant isolation, per-tenant feature flags, and data-minimization, ensuring privacy-preserving analytics across tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant feature flags
- Data-minimization in scoring
- Deterministic model versions and seeds
- Rate quotas and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
function preparePayload(payload, tenantConfig) {
  if (!tenantConfig.flags.include_personal_data) {
    delete payload.device_id;
    delete payload.user_id;
  }
  return payload;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit flag changes across tenants safely?
- How would you test determinism if flags change mid-rollout?
